package conformance

// AuditVector is one serialised AuditObservation and whether it is acceptable.
//
// THE SUBJECT IS THE RAW JSON, NOT A STRUCT, because the receiver decodes the
// subtree by itself and a type error (a port of 70000, a count that is a
// string) must cost it the same thing a validation error does: the subtree and
// nothing else. A peer holds the vector to the whole path:
//
//	var observation protocol.AuditObservation
//	err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(vector.JSON), &observation)
//	if err == nil {
//		err = protocol.ValidateAuditObservation(observation)
//	}
//	// err == nil exactly when vector.Valid
//
// A node builds rows, not JSON, so it holds its per-row checks to the same
// table by encoding what it builds; the point of the table is that the
// accept and reject sets are written down once.
//
// The expected values are written from the rules, not copied from the code:
// each name states the rule it pins. Append to the table; never edit a verdict
// to make an implementation pass.
type AuditVector struct {
	Name  string
	JSON  string
	Valid bool
}

